<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    THE PRODUCT'S TOKEN LAYER. One file, and before it existed there were none: App.axaml was
    eight lines of bare FluentTheme, and across every .axaml under client/src there were zero
    ResourceDictionary, zero StaticResource and zero DynamicResource. Colour was pasted as hex at
    342 sites, 102 of them the same ink.

    THE SHAPE IS WPF'S OWN, deliberately and for one reason: the shipping product re-themes at
    runtime. `RefreshThemeAwareElements` rewrites `Application.Current.Resources` by KEY - the
    Colour entry and its Brush sibling, both - every time the active mod changes
    (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1611-1682), and everything downstream repaints because it
    referenced the key rather than a value. So this file is Colours first, Brushes derived from
    them, and every consumer binds the BRUSH dynamically.

    ====================== THIS FILE IS A SEED. IT IS NOT WHAT A USER SEES. ======================
    That was true of the file it was copied from and it is now true of this one in the same way.
    Themes/CcpTheme.cs carries the built-in "CCP Default" mod's own six colours and App.Initialize
    paints them over the keys below before a single window exists - which is exactly what the
    shipping product does, at MainWindow.xaml.cs:317. Eight of the keys in this file are therefore
    DESIGN-TIME VALUES that no running application ever resolves:

      DarkerBg, SurfaceBg, PanelBg          <- the mod's Background / Surface / Panel colours
      PanelAccent, PanelAccentHover         <- DERIVED at apply time, LightenColor(panel, .15/.25)
      PinkColor, ShellAccent, ShellAccentBright, and Fluent's seven accent keys

    Everything else below IS what a user sees, because a ModTheme supplies six colours and nothing
    more: ElevatedSurface, SeatBg, the three inks, NeonPurple and both semantics are served straight
    off this file. That asymmetry is upstream's own and it is measurable on its window - see
    CcpTheme's header for the counts.

    KEEP THE SEED IN STEP WITH UPSTREAM'S ANYWAY, and ThemeTokenTests does. Both products carry a
    seed and paint over it; a seed that drifted would move the keys a mod does not supply, and would
    move where the next re-theme lands.

    ================================ THE VALUES ARE UPSTREAM'S ================================
    The port used to be violet-tinted where the shipping product is navy-and-pink, and the owner's
    requirement is that the two look the same. As of this file's second revision every colour below
    is the shipping product's own value for the same role, cited to the line that declares it, and
    the port's former value is kept beside it so the flip stays reviewable.

    WHY IT LANDED IN TWO COMMITS AND NOT ONE. The sweep that created this file was deliberately
    value-preserving, because nine files read the port's exact bytes back off real pixels or real
    markup (client/tools/verify/checks.json, RackPresentationTests, TrainerCardTests,
    TrainerCardRecordPresentationTests, CompanionPrivacyPresentationTests,
    NavigationShellHeadlessTests and their siblings). Recolouring while tokenising would have been
    two changes wearing one commit; the tokens bought the flip, and the flip was one line per role
    here plus the re-derived tolerances there, instead of 342 scattered edits.

    THE THREE PINKS ARE NOT A GUESS. The seed dictionary's accent ladder is named twice over:
    Colors.xaml declares PinkColor #FFFF69B4 (:5), DarkPink #FFFF1493 (:6) and PinkButtonHovered
    #FFFF8FAF (:34), and RefreshThemeAwareElements names the SAME three as accent / dark / light in
    its own fallbacks (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1565-1567) before rewriting those three
    keys from whatever the active mod supplies (:1655-1657). The port's three accent keys take that
    triad in that order.

    ================== THE MEASUREMENT THAT MADE THIS FILE A SEED, RE-RUN ==================
    Taken 2026-08-25 and re-measured independently on 2026-08-26 before the theme layer was built:
    the shipping product, built from this repository, run headed against a throwaway
    CCP_USERDATA_DIR so no mod but the shipping default was installed (its own selector read "CCP
    Default"). Exact-hex counts over the whole 2735x1650 window:

      #08080C 27.37%, #0C0C13 9.21%, #11111A 13.93%, #E84393 3.07%, #B83078 0.10%
      #34343C  1.28%  <- LightenColor(#11111A, .15), a colour in NO dictionary of either product
      #222240  5.13%  <- ElevatedSurface, THIS FILE'S VALUE, because no ModTheme supplies it
      #121220 32px, #181830 7px, #2E2E4A 9px, #3A3A5C 0, #7A7A94 0, #FFFF1493 0, #FFFF8FAF 0

    Four of this file's grounds are down at single-digit PIXEL counts on a 4.5-million-pixel window;
    the mod's six and the two derived from them are most of what is on it. The port now applies the
    same skin over the same seed, which is what the FIRST five rows of counts are the target for.

    ONE ROW OF THAT MEASUREMENT IS AN UPSTREAM QUIRK AND THE PORT DOES NOT REPRODUCE IT: #1C1C35 -
    this file's PanelBg, and a key a ModTheme DOES supply - survives at 2.86% of that window, all of
    it the bottom action bar and a few borders. MainWindow.xaml:2463 binds {StaticResource
    PanelBgBrush} where :285 binds {DynamicResource PanelBgBrush}, and only the second follows the
    rewrite. That is a WPF resource-lookup artefact, not a design decision - it leaves upstream's
    START/Save/Exit bar a different shade from every other panel on the screen - and porting the
    mechanics of a bug is not porting an outcome.

    FLUENT'S ACCENT FAMILY at the foot of this file had no port value to preserve in the first
    place. It resolved from the user's Windows personalisation setting, so the product's checkboxes
    and sliders were Windows blue in a pink application AND A DIFFERENT COLOUR ON EVERY MACHINE.
    There is nothing to conserve in that, so it takes the shipping product's accent verbatim - and
    the theme rewrites all seven, because a skin that moved only the base would leave every hover,
    pressed and disabled state on the previous accent.

    ONE GAP THIS FILE CANNOT CLOSE AND IS NOT ALLOWED TO PRETEND ABOUT: upstream's START button is
    a GRADIENT, #E84393 -> #8B5CF6 (Resources/Theme/Brushes.xaml:222-225, activated at the four
    brand anchors when the active mod is CCP Default, MainWindow.xaml.cs:1687-1690), and this port
    paints it flat. That is a brush SHAPE, not a token value; no colour written below can produce
    it and nothing here should be bent trying.
  -->

  <!-- ================================= GROUNDS AND SURFACES ================================= -->

  <!-- The application ground: the shell window's own Background, and the page ground behind
       every card. WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:9 DarkerBg #FF121220. Was #FF141018. -->
  <Color x:Key="DarkerBg">#FF121220</Color>

  <!-- Structural chrome that frames content rather than carrying it: the nav rail, the Studio
       rack, the diagnostic footer, the session action bar.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:10 SurfaceBg #FF181830. Was #FF19141F. -->
  <Color x:Key="SurfaceBg">#FF181830</Color>

  <!-- A card that carries content: the module panel, the Play hero.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:7 PanelBg #FF1C1C35. Was #FF1B1622. -->
  <Color x:Key="PanelBg">#FF1C1C35</Color>

  <!-- A seat a control sits in: a rail door at rest, a companion dial seat. THE ONE COLOUR BELOW
       THAT IS NOT A LINE OF UPSTREAM'S, because upstream has no key for this tier - it seats
       controls on SurfaceBg. The port's own tier is kept (removing it would flatten the rail door
       onto the rack), and it is DERIVED rather than invented: the arithmetic midpoint of the two
       upstream tiers it sits between, PanelBg #FF1C1C35 (Colors.xaml:7) and ElevatedSurface
       #FF222240 (Colors.xaml:71), which is where the port's old value sat in the port's old ramp.
       Was #FF1E1822. -->
  <Color x:Key="SeatBg">#FF1F1F3A</Color>

  <!-- One step above a panel: a notice, a badge, a hovered rack row.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:71 ElevatedSurface #FF222240. Was #FF241E2A. -->
  <Color x:Key="ElevatedSurface">#FF222240</Color>

  <!-- The interactive tier: a selected row's fill, a secondary button's face, a progress track.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:19 PanelAccent #FF2E2E4A. Was #FF2A2130. -->
  <Color x:Key="PanelAccent">#FF2E2E4A</Color>

  <!-- A border at rest, and the hairline between chrome regions.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:20 PanelAccentHover #FF3A3A5C. Was #FF3A2F3E. -->
  <Color x:Key="PanelAccentHover">#FF3A3A5C</Color>

  <!-- ======================================== INK ======================================== -->

  <!-- Primary ink. The single most-pasted literal in the product before this file existed: 102
       sites, 48 of them in StudioPage.axaml alone.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:11 TextLight #FFF0F0F5. Was #FFE8E0EE. -->
  <Color x:Key="TextLight">#FFF0F0F5</Color>

  <!-- Secondary ink: description lines, units, hints.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:15 TextMuted #FFA0A0BC. Was #FF9A8FA3. -->
  <Color x:Key="TextMuted">#FFA0A0BC</Color>

  <!-- Decorative-only, and it stays that way: never carry information in TextDim. Also the
       pointer-over border and the rack's off/armed state dot.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:74 TextDim #FF7A7A94. Was #FF6B5B73. -->
  <Color x:Key="TextDim">#FF7A7A94</Color>

  <!-- NO TOKEN FOR WHITE, and that is a decision rather than an omission. The product paints
       #FFFFFFFF at five sites and they do not share a role: three are ink on an accent fill, one
       is the Play hero's title on a dark card, one is a picker handle. A "TextOnAccent" key would
       have to be pasted at the two that are not on an accent to keep the sweep mechanical, and a
       token whose name is wrong at 40% of its uses is worse than the literal it replaced. White
       is not a palette decision; it is white. -->

  <!-- ====================================== ACCENTS ======================================

       THREE accent colours, because the product really paints three - and the seed dictionary
       declares exactly three too, which is the flip's luckiest fact. RefreshThemeAwareElements
       reads an accent, a DARK companion and a LIGHT one, and when nothing supplies them it falls
       back to #FF69B4 / #FF1493 / #FF8FAF (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1565-1567) - the same
       three Colors.xaml declares at :5, :6 and :34. The keys below are that ladder in that order.
       They are the SEED's ladder and not the skin a user sees; the measured gap is in the header
       block above, under WHAT THIS FILE MATCHES.

       For the later mod-re-theme capability: upstream's rewrite targets the keys `PinkColor`,
       `DarkPink` and `PinkButtonHovered` (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1655-1657), so a mod
       reaching into this dictionary by the first of those names repaints the Fluent accent and the
       info toast. The shell's own two accents are separate keys HERE - `ShellAccent` is upstream's
       DarkPink slot and `ShellAccentBright` is its PinkButtonHovered slot - and that mapping is
       what that feature's own table will need. Recorded here so the work discovers it from the
       file rather than from a capture.

       AND WHY `ShellAccent` IS NOT SIMPLY `PinkColor`, since upstream's START button really is
       PinkBrush. Two token keys may hold the same value, but two CHECKS may not: the pop quiz
       card's question ink is upstream's own COLORREF 0x00B469FF = #FF69B4
       (Input/Win32InputPresence), it is declared in client/tools/verify/checks.json at tolerance
       24, and PopQuizCardPresentationTests refuses any other surface's check within that distance
       - a separation of zero is not fixable by any tolerance. So the shell's CTA livery takes the
       ladder's DARK step, which is the pink upstream paints on an active or hovered pink surface
       (WPF Resources/Theme/Controls.xaml:57, :352, :409), and the two stay 85 apart.
  -->

  <!-- Upstream's accent, verbatim, and already the port's own value at the info toast
       (ToastHost.axaml) and the Studio picker. This is also what Fluent's accent family below
       resolves to. WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:5 PinkColor #FFFF69B4 - EXACT MATCH. -->
  <Color x:Key="PinkColor">#FFFF69B4</Color>

  <!-- The shell's accent: the session START button's idle livery, the module action rim, the
       intake gate, the trainer card's XP fill. WPF's own START is PinkBrush
       (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.StartStop.cs:779), so the ROLE is upstream's base accent; the
       VALUE is the ladder's dark step for the reason stated in the block above.
       WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1566 darkHex #FF1493 = Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:6
       DarkPink. Was #FFD05CE8. -->
  <Color x:Key="ShellAccent">#FFFF1493</Color>

  <!-- The bright accent: SELECTION and LIVE. Rail door checked border, rack row selection
       marker, the rack's live state dot.

       THE HEADED HARNESS'S SEEDED-REGRESSION ANCHOR MOVED OFF THIS LINE, and it had to. The value
       below is a SEED: CcpTheme rewrites `ShellAccentBright` from the active theme's
       AccentLightColor before any window exists, so a wrong colour seeded here would be painted
       over three lines into startup and would reach no pixel at all - the self-test would restore
       a green build and report that its own regression detector had stopped working. The anchor is
       the AccentLight line of CcpTheme.CcpDefault now, which is where the value a user SEES comes
       from; self-test.ps1 seeds THAT and still requires both `rail-door-selected-border` and
       `rack-row-selected-marker` to trip on real pixels.

       The claim is unchanged and still stronger than the markup it replaced: the seed reaches every
       consumer of the key, present and future, because the colour exists in exactly one place.
       client/tools/verify/checks.json reads the two checks back as #FF6FB5.

       WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1567 lightHex #FF8FAF = Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:34
       PinkButtonHovered. Was #FFE066FF. -->
  <Color x:Key="ShellAccentBright">#FFFF8FAF</Color>

  <!-- The tier-2 / tier-3 entitlement rim and its refusal plate.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:129 NeonPurple #FFB47BFF - EXACT MATCH. -->
  <Color x:Key="NeonPurple">#FFB47BFF</Color>

  <!-- ===================================== SEMANTICS =====================================
       Severity, not brand. These stay put when the accent moves, exactly as upstream's Danger
       and SuccessGreen do (WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:23-28 say so in their own words).
  -->

  <!-- A session that is RUNNING repaints the one START button red. Upstream builds this in code
       as Color.FromRgb(255,107,107) (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.StartStop.cs:756) - so the value
       is upstream's exactly, and only the key is the port's.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:23 Danger #FFE53935 is upstream's other red, for dialogs. -->
  <Color x:Key="Danger">#FFFF6B6B</Color>

  <!-- The FAILURE livery, which exists to be told apart at a glance from the two REFUSAL
       liveries above. Upstream never needs it: its failure surface is a modal MessageBox with
       MessageBoxImage.Warning (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.Lab.cs:164-165), a different WINDOW.
       The port reuses the plate idiom on the same page, so the severity signal has to be
       carried by the colour instead of by the window. -->
  <Color x:Key="Warning">#FFF0A02E</Color>

  <!-- ====================================== BRUSHES ======================================
       Derived from the colours above through DynamicResource, which is upstream's own structure
       and its own stated reason: "use DynamicResource so runtime color changes propagate"
       (WPF Resources/Theme/Brushes.xaml:4). Consumers bind the BRUSH; nothing in the product
       binds a Color except a gradient stop.
  -->

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DarkerBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource DarkerBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource SurfaceBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PanelBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SeatBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource SeatBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ElevatedSurfaceBrush" Color="{DynamicResource ElevatedSurface}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelAccentBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PanelAccent}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelAccentHoverBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PanelAccentHover}" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextLightBrush" Color="{DynamicResource TextLight}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextMutedBrush" Color="{DynamicResource TextMuted}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDimBrush" Color="{DynamicResource TextDim}" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PinkBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PinkColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ShellAccentBrush" Color="{DynamicResource ShellAccent}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ShellAccentBrightBrush" Color="{DynamicResource ShellAccentBright}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NeonPurpleBrush" Color="{DynamicResource NeonPurple}" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerBrush" Color="{DynamicResource Danger}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningBrush" Color="{DynamicResource Warning}" />

  <!-- ==================================== TYPOGRAPHY ====================================
       The product had NO FontFamily anywhere before this line: six occurrences across the whole
       tree, every one a Consolas probe inside a web-host window. Upstream sets Segoe UI on the
       Window and inherits it everywhere (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml:24).

       THE FALLBACK CHAIN IS NOT DECORATION. Segoe UI does not exist on Linux, and this client
       ships there. Noto Sans and DejaVu Sans are the two faces a desktop Linux install can be
       relied on to carry; the trailing generic hands the last word to the platform's own font
       manager rather than to whatever Avalonia happens to pick first.
  -->
  <FontFamily x:Key="CcpFontFamily">Segoe UI, Noto Sans, DejaVu Sans, sans-serif</FontFamily>

  <!-- ================================== GEOMETRY SCALE ==================================
       The radii the shell really uses, stated once. Adopted by the shell's own style block
       (Views/MainWindow.axaml); per-page one-off geometry is still written inline.
  -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusChip">4</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControl">6</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPanel">8</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPlate">10</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusGate">12</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPill">14</CornerRadius>
  <!-- 15 is 16 minus the card's 1px border, which is where upstream derives it
       (WPF Views/Tabs/PlayTabView.xaml:252). A band inside RadiusCard must use this. -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusBand">15</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusCard">16</CornerRadius>

  <!-- The uniform paddings. Non-uniform ones stay inline: a "12,6" is a layout decision at one
       site, not a token, and hiding it behind a name would cost a lookup and buy nothing. -->
  <Thickness x:Key="PadRack">8</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PadNotice">12</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PadPanel">16</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PadCard">24</Thickness>

  <!-- ============================== FLUENT'S ACCENT FAMILY ==============================

       THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES, measured on captured pixels before it existed: every unstyled
       Fluent control resolved its accent from the user's Windows personalisation setting
       (HKCU\...\Explorer\Accent\AccentColorMenu). The port's checkboxes and sliders measured
       #0078D4 - 3,500 exact-hex pixels in a port capture and ZERO in the same capture of the
       shipping product - so the most-touched controls in a magenta application were Windows
       blue, and a different blue on every machine.

       Application.Resources is consulted BEFORE the theme's own dictionaries, so declaring the
       seven keys here shadows Fluent's without forking a control theme or restating a template.
       All seven are written out rather than left to Fluent's shade calculator: the calculator
       runs off the platform accent, which is the thing being removed.

       The shades are the standard Fluent ladder around the base - Light1/2/3 lift toward white,
       Dark1/2/3 toward black - so hover, pressed and disabled states stay distinguishable
       instead of collapsing onto one flat pink.

       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:5 PinkColor #FFFF69B4 is the base, and upstream paints the
       same accent on the same controls: its 18-unit circle checkbox takes PinkBrush on hover and
       on check (WPF Resources/Theme/Inputs.xaml:218-262).
  -->
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColor">#FFFF69B4</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight1">#FFFF85C2</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight2">#FFFFA1D0</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight3">#FFFFBDDE</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark1">#FFD9599A</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark2">#FFB34A80</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark3">#FF8C3A66</Color>

</ResourceDictionary>
